    "result_text": "Done. The test suite is green (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe spec adds a derived `riskFlag` to the owner representation, alongside the existing derived signals (`possibleDuplicate`, `capacityWarning`, `membership...`). I followed the repo's established pattern of a small static \"mark\" helper invoked from the responders.\n\n**Files touched (4):**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `riskFlag` boolean property to the `Owner` schema (regenerates `OwnerDto` with `get/setRiskFlag`).\n\n2. **`RiskFlag.java`** (new) \u2014 a small cohesive helper. `mark(dto, owner)` sets `riskFlag = possibleDuplicate || capacityWarning || disposable-adjacent-email`. It reads the two flags the responder already derived (no repository re-scan, so it mirrors exactly what `PossibleDuplicate`/`CapacityWarning` reported), and computes email adjacency by matching a domain's second-level label against known disposable services.\n\n3. **`RespondWithOwner.java`** (GET) \u2014 one added line: `RiskFlag.mark(dto, owner);`.\n\n4. **`RespondWithOwnerCreated.java`** (POST) \u2014 one added line, for consistency wherever the sibling flags are set.\n\n## Structural-impact notes\n\nEach responder is a single-method class (`WMC_other = 0`), and each edit is one added line at CC 1, so the per-function cost stays near the floor. I deliberately avoided touching the large derivation classes or growing any existing method \u2014 the interpretive logic lives in the new small unit, and the two wire-ins reuse the already-computed DTO flags rather than duplicating the duplicate/capacity queries.\n\nInterpretation choices (unasserted by the single provided test, which only checks `riskFlag == false` for a normal owner): \"over its soft capacity\" maps to the existing 40\u201349 `capacityWarning` band; \"disposable-adjacent\" means the domain's second-level label matches a known disposable service (a superset-style match beyond the exact blocklist that `RejectDisposableEmail` rejects at create time).",
